//? Title: Beam Search
//? Subtitle: Bounded-memory path planning on the Maunga Whau elevation map with datamunge's algorithms::terrain_beam_search, from greedy descent to an optimal label-correcting search as the beam widens
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Beam search keeps only the \(B\) most promising nodes of each layer of a search and forgets the
rest, so its memory is bounded by the beam width rather than the size of the graph. We state the
algorithm with a cost bound that makes it anytime. We then prove the two ends of its range:
\(B=1\) is a greedy walk that can trap itself, and unlimited \(B\) is a label-correcting
Bellman–Ford search that is optimal for any admissible heuristic. On the Maunga Whau elevation
map, the same problem as the A* report, a beam of 16 cells finds the optimal route while
holding only 16 cells per layer. Narrower beams return routes up to 26% dearer or none at all.
Wide beams expand more cells than A*, because they re-expand cells whose cost improves.
)

\toc

> The method

>> Layers and a beam

Beam search (Lowerre 1976) explores a graph in layers, like breadth-first search, but keeps
only the \(B\) most promising nodes of each layer, the *beam*. Each beam node's neighbours are
generated and ranked, and only the best \(B\) form the next layer. Everything else is forgotten.
The memory held at any time is one layer, at most \(B\) nodes, whatever the size of the graph.

`algorithms::terrain_beam_search()` runs this on the terrain grids used in the A* report. Each
cell \(v\) carries the cheapest cost \(g(v)\) found so far and a parent pointer. Let \(h\) be an
admissible heuristic (a lower bound on the remaining cost), and \(U\) the cost of the best path to
the goal \(t\) found so far (initially \(\infty\)). One layer works as follows:

```
next <- {}
for u in beam:
    for each neighbour v of u:
        g' <- g(u) + c(u, v)
        if g' >= g(v) or g' + h(v) >= U: skip        # not better, or cannot beat U
        g(v) <- g'; parent(v) <- u
        if v == t: U <- g'                          # new best path, t is not expanded
        else: add v to next
drop from next every v with g(v) + h(v) >= U
rank next by key(v) and keep the first B             # key = g + h, or h alone
beam <- next
```

The search stops when the beam is empty, and the best path is read from the parent pointers.
Classic beam search stops as soon as the goal is first reached (`stop_at_first`); continuing
with the bound \(U\) turns it into an *anytime* search whose answer can only improve.

Two rankings are available. With \(\text{key}=g+h\) the beam keeps the cells that look
cheapest end to end, as A* would. With \(\text{key}=h\) it keeps the cells that look closest to
the goal, ignoring what it cost to reach them: this is greedy best-first search with a
bounded frontier.

>> The two extremes

**Width one.** With \(B=1\) the beam is a single cell, and each layer moves to its best-ranked
neighbour that improves on any earlier visit. With \(\text{key}=h\) this is steepest descent on
\(h\), and it can stop in a cell all of whose neighbours were reached more cheaply before, a
dead end. The search uses \(O(1)\) memory beyond the cost table and follows one path.

**Unlimited width.** If \(B\) is at least the number of cells, nothing is ever cut, and the
search becomes a label-correcting shortest-path method: each layer relaxes the out-edges of
exactly the cells whose \(g\) improved in the previous one. This is the queue form of the
Bellman–Ford algorithm, with the extra rule that discards cells with \(g+h\ge U\). It is optimal.

*Proof.* Let \(s=v_0,v_1,\dots,v_m=t\) be an optimal path of cost \(C^*\), and suppose the search
ends with \(U>C^*\). Every prefix of the optimal path is optimal, so \(g^*(v_i)+h(v_i)\le
g^*(v_i)+h^*(v_i)=C^*<U\) for every \(i\), using admissibility \(h\le h^*\). Hence no \(v_i\) with
\(g(v_i)=g^*(v_i)\) is ever discarded by the bound. Take the largest \(i<m\) for which \(g(v_i)\) reaches
\(g^*(v_i)\) at some point; \(i=0\) qualifies, since \(g(s)=0\). In the layer where that happens,
\(v_i\) enters the next beam (no cell is cut when \(B\) is unlimited), and when it is expanded,

$$
g^*(v_i)+c(v_i,v_{i+1})=g^*(v_{i+1})<g(v_{i+1})\quad\text{and}\quad g^*(v_{i+1})+h(v_{i+1})\le C^*<U,
$$
\alttext(g star of v i plus c of v i, v i plus 1 equals g star of v i plus 1, which is less than g of v i plus 1; and g star of v i plus 1 plus h of v i plus 1 is at most C star, which is less than U.)

so \(g(v_{i+1})\) is set to \(g^*(v_{i+1})\), contradicting the choice of \(i\). (If \(v_{i+1}=t\),
\(U\) drops to \(C^*\).) So \(U=C^*\) at the end. \(\blacksquare\)

The proof uses only one property of unlimited width: no cell on the optimal path is ever cut.
With a finite beam, the optimal path survives only if each of its cells ranks among the best
\(B\) of its layer. When one is cut, it may never be reached again, and the search returns a worse
path or none at all. Beam search with finite \(B\) is therefore *incomplete* and *not optimal*,
and the width trades memory and time against the quality of the answer. The bound \(U\) still
gives a guarantee: the returned cost is the cost of a real path, so it is an upper bound on
\(C^*\), while the A* lower bound \(h(s)\le C^*\) brackets it from below.

> The terrain

We use the same problem as the A* report. `dm::datasets::volcano()` is the topography of Maunga
Whau, Auckland, on an \(87\times61\) grid with 10 m spacing. A step costs its horizontal length plus
\(\lambda=5000/600\approx8.3\) for every metre climbed (Naismith's rule), and the walker crosses
the map from the south-east foot to the north-west foot. The heuristic is the octile distance
plus \(\lambda\) times the net height still to gain, which is consistent. Dijkstra's algorithm
gives the optimum \(C^*\) for comparison.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/beam_paths.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(dm::datasets::volcano(), "x", "y", "elevation", 5000.0 / 600.0);
  const std::size_t start = 80 * grid.cols + 5, goal = 5 * grid.cols + 55;
  const auto kind = alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb;
  const auto optimal = alg::terrain_a_star(grid, start, goal, kind);
  std::print("Optimal cost C* = {:.1f} along {} cells; A* expanded {} cells.\n\n", optimal.cost, optimal.path.size(),
             optimal.expansion_order.size());

  const auto coords = [&](const std::vector<std::size_t>& cells) {
    std::pair<std::vector<double>, std::vector<double>> xy;
    for (const std::size_t c : cells) {
      xy.first.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c / grid.cols));
      xy.second.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c % grid.cols));
    }
    return xy;
  };
  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.rows; ++i) xs.push_back(grid.cell_size * i);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.cols; ++j) ys.push_back(grid.cell_size * j);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(grid.rows, std::vector<double>(grid.cols));
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < grid.elevation.size(); ++c) z[c / grid.cols][c % grid.cols] = grid.elevation[c];
  const auto [ox, oy] = coords(optimal.path);

  std::print("| width \\(B\\) | first path cost | layers to first | final cost | final / \\(C^*\\) | cells expanded |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  for (const std::size_t width : {1UL, 4UL, 16UL, 64UL}) {
    const auto beam = alg::terrain_beam_search(grid, start, goal, kind, width);
    const auto [ex, ey] = coords(beam.best.expansion_order);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                    .image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::terrain_colors(20))
                    .points(ex, ey, "", dm::RGB{30, 64, 175}, 2.0)
                    .lines(ox, oy, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.5)
                    .x_label("x (m)")
                    .y_label("y (m)");
    if (!beam.best.found) {
      std::print("| {} | no path | – | no path | – | {} |\n", width, beam.best.expansion_order.size());
      plot.title(std::format("B = {}: no path ({} layers)", width, beam.layers));
    } else {
      std::print("| {} | {:.1f} | {} | {:.1f} | {:.3f} | {} |\n", width, beam.first_cost, beam.first_layer,
                 beam.best.cost, beam.best.cost / optimal.cost, beam.best.expansion_order.size());
      const auto [px, py] = coords(beam.best.path);
      plot.lines(px, py, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.5)
          .title(std::format("B = {}: cost {:.1f} ({:+.1f}%)", width, beam.best.cost,
                             100.0 * (beam.best.cost / optimal.cost - 1.0)));
    }
    layout.add(plot);
  }
  layout.size(1200, 900).save_svg("build/beam_paths.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Optimal cost C* = 1291.5 along 99 cells; A* expanded 1732 cells.

| width \(B\) | first path cost | layers to first | final cost | final / \(C^*\) | cells expanded |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | no path | – | no path | – | 54 |
| 4 | 1627.2 | 117 | 1627.2 | 1.260 | 459 |
| 16 | 1291.5 | 97 | 1291.5 | 1.000 | 1525 |
| 64 | 1291.5 | 80 | 1291.5 | 1.000 | 4109 |
\image(figures/beam_paths.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Cells expanded (blue dots) by beam searches of width 1, 4, 16 and 64, on the elevation map. The red line is the best path each found; the thin black line is the optimal path.)
\alttext(Four panels of the coloured elevation map, 860 by 600 metres, each with the thin black optimal path running north from the start at the bottom right, then north-west along the top edge to the goal at the top left. B equals 1: a single row of blue dots runs west along the bottom edge from the start and stops near x equals 320 metres; no red path. B equals 4: a red path runs west along the southern foot, round the cone's west side and north to the goal, with blue dots hugging it. B equals 16: the red path coincides with the black optimal path; blue dots fill a band along the northern route and a second band along the south. B equals 64: same red path; blue dots cover most of the low ground and part of the cone.)

The optimal route costs \(C^*=1291.5\) and runs north from the start, then west along the top of
the map, skirting the cone. A* with the same heuristic expands 1,732 cells to prove it.

With \(B=1\) the search fails. Ranked by \(g+h\), the single beam cell heads west along the low
southern edge of the map, where moves cost no climbing. After 54 layers, at \(x=320\) m on the
map edge, it reaches a cell whose four unexpanded neighbours had all been generated earlier at a
lower cost and then cut from the beam. Since a cell is kept only when its cost improves, none
of them can re-enter, and the beam empties. This is the incompleteness of the proof above: the
search remembers the costs of the cells it discarded, but not the cells themselves.

With \(B=4\) the beam follows the southern route round the cone and reaches the goal after 117
layers, at a cost 26% above the optimum. The beam is wide enough to stay alive but not to
explore the northern route at the same time. With \(B=16\) both routes survive side by side in
the beam, and the northern one reaches the goal first, after 97 layers, at exactly \(C^*\). The
bound \(U\) then cuts off the southern band, so the search expands 1,525 cells, fewer than A*'s
1,732. With \(B=64\) the answer is the same but the beam spreads over most of the low ground and
expands 4,109 cells.

> Width against quality and work

We now sweep the width over powers of two, from 1 to 8192 (more than the 5,307 cells), with
both rankings, and record the first and final path costs, the cells expanded, the largest beam
and the time taken.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/beam_sweep.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(dm::datasets::volcano(), "x", "y", "elevation", 5000.0 / 600.0);
  const std::size_t start = 80 * grid.cols + 5, goal = 5 * grid.cols + 55;
  const auto kind = alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb;
  const auto optimal = alg::terrain_a_star(grid, start, goal, kind);
  const double c_star = optimal.cost;

  std::print("| ranking | \\(B\\) | first / \\(C^*\\) | final / \\(C^*\\) | expanded | largest beam | layers | time (ms) |\n"
             "| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  struct Series {
    std::vector<double> log_b, first, final_cost, expanded;
  };
  std::map<std::string, Series> series;
  for (const auto& [name, ranking] : {std::pair{"g + h", alg::BeamRanking::CostPlusHeuristic},
                                      std::pair{"h only", alg::BeamRanking::Heuristic}}) {
    auto& s = series[name];
    for (std::size_t width = 1; width <= 8192; width *= 2) {
      const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
      const auto beam = alg::terrain_beam_search(grid, start, goal, kind, width, ranking);
      const double ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
      if (!beam.best.found) {
        std::print("| {} | {} | no path | no path | {} | {} | {} | {:.2f} |\n", name, width,
                   beam.best.expansion_order.size(), beam.max_beam, beam.layers, ms);
        continue;
      }
      s.log_b.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(width)));
      s.first.push_back(beam.first_cost / c_star);
      s.final_cost.push_back(beam.best.cost / c_star);
      s.expanded.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(beam.best.expansion_order.size())));
      if (std::has_single_bit(width) && (width <= 64 || width >= 1024 || width == 256))
        std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | {} | {} | {} | {:.2f} |\n", name, width, beam.first_cost / c_star,
                   beam.best.cost / c_star, beam.best.expansion_order.size(), beam.max_beam, beam.layers, ms);
    }
  }
  std::print("\nA* with the same heuristic expands {} cells.\n", optimal.expansion_order.size());

  const dm::RGB blue{37, 99, 235}, orange{217, 119, 6};
  const auto& a = series["g + h"];
  const auto& b = series["h only"];
  auto quality = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                     .lines(a.log_b, a.final_cost, "g + h, final", blue, 2.0)
                     .points(a.log_b, a.first, "g + h, first path", blue, 5.0)
                     .lines(b.log_b, b.final_cost, "h only, final", orange, 2.0)
                     .points(b.log_b, b.first, "h only, first path", orange, 5.0)
                     .abline_h(1.0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
                     .title("Path cost relative to the optimum")
                     .x_label("log2 of beam width B")
                     .y_label("cost / C*");
  const double a_star_cells = std::log10(static_cast<double>(optimal.expansion_order.size()));
  auto work = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .lines(a.log_b, a.expanded, "g + h", blue, 2.0)
                  .points(a.log_b, a.expanded, "", blue, 5.0)
                  .lines(b.log_b, b.expanded, "h only", orange, 2.0)
                  .points(b.log_b, b.expanded, "", orange, 5.0)
                  .abline_h(a_star_cells, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
                  .title("Cells expanded (line: A*)")
                  .x_label("log2 of beam width B")
                  .y_label("log10 cells expanded");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(quality).add(work).size(1200, 460).save_svg("build/beam_sweep.svg");
}
```
