\image(figures/beam_sweep.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: cost of the first and the final path found, relative to the optimum, against the beam width. Right: cells expanded, on a log scale, with A*'s count as a horizontal line.)
\alttext(Two panels with log2 of the beam width from 0 to 13 on the x-axis. Left: cost relative to the optimum. The blue g plus h series starts at log2 B equals 2 at 1.26, falls to 1.24 at 3, and is exactly 1 from 4 onwards; its first-path dots lie on the line. The orange h-only final line starts at 1.167, wanders down between 1.14 and 1.15, drops to 1.08 at 6, and reaches 1 at 8 and beyond, while its first-path dots stay at 1.154 for every width from 1 up. Right: log10 cells expanded. Both lines rise roughly linearly with log2 B and level off: blue at about 3.74 (5,439 cells) from log2 B equals 7, orange at about 4.07 (11,885 cells) from 8. A horizontal line at 3.24 marks A*'s 1,732 cells; both curves cross it between log2 B equals 4 and 5.)

Ranking by \(g+h\) fails for \(B\le2\), is 24–26% above the optimum for \(B=4\) and 8, and is
optimal for every \(B\ge16\). For this ranking the first path found is already the final one:
once the goal is reached, the bound removes everything else in the beam.

Ranking by \(h\) alone never fails. The beam always moves toward the goal, so even \(B=1\)
arrives, at 16.7% above the optimum, in 75 layers. But the first path it finds is 15.4% above
the optimum for every width from 2 to 8192, because \(h\) ignores the climbing already done and
the first beam cell to reach the goal is the one that came most directly. The anytime
continuation is what improves it: after the first path, the bound \(U\) prunes the beam and later
layers find cheaper routes, reaching \(C^*\) from \(B=256\).

The work grows almost in proportion to \(B\) until the beam stops being cut. From \(B=256\) the
largest beam actually held is 145 cells for \(g+h\) and 310 for \(h\), and every wider beam
does the same search. That unlimited search expands 5,439 cells for \(g+h\), more than the 5,307
cells in the grid, and 11,885 for \(h\). The reason is the Bellman–Ford structure of the
proof: a layered search can reach a cell cheaply only after reaching it expensively, and every
improvement re-expands the cell. A*'s priority queue avoids this by always expanding the
cheapest \(f\) first, so with a consistent heuristic each cell is expanded once. The beam's
advantage is memory. With \(B=16\) it holds 16 cells per layer and still finds the optimum,
whereas A* pushes 3,571 entries onto its queue for the same answer. All searches take under a millisecond on this
machine.

> Summary

- Beam search keeps the best \(B\) cells per layer, so memory is \(O(B)\) per layer, but it is
  incomplete and not optimal for finite \(B\). `algorithms::terrain_beam_search()` adds the cost
  bound \(U\), so after the first path it keeps searching for cheaper ones until the beam empties.
- With \(B=1\) it is a greedy walk that can trap itself; with unlimited \(B\) it is a
  label-correcting Bellman–Ford search, optimal for any admissible heuristic, as proved above.
- On Maunga Whau, ranking by \(g+h\) fails for \(B\le2\), is about 25% off at \(B=4\) or 8, and is
  optimal from \(B=16\), where it expands fewer cells than A*. Ranking by \(h\) never fails, but its
  first path stays 15% off at every width; only the anytime bound brings it to the optimum.
- Unlimited beams expand more cells than A*, up to 2.2 times the grid for the \(h\) ranking,
  because layered search re-expands cells whose cost improves. Beam search trades that extra
  work for bounded memory.

> References

- Lowerre, B. T. (1976). *The Harpy Speech Recognition System*. PhD thesis, Carnegie Mellon
  University.
- Zhou, R. and Hansen, E. A. (2005). Beam-stack search: integrating backtracking with beam
  search. *Proceedings of ICAPS 2005*, 90–98.
- Bellman, R. (1958). On a routing problem. *Quarterly of Applied Mathematics* 16, 87–90.
- Ihaka, R. (1996). The `volcano` dataset in R: topographic information on Auckland's Maunga
  Whau volcano.
